<!-- An ensō crossed by a loaded bar. Same drawing as www/icon.svg — keep the two in step.

     The ensō (円相) is the Zen brush circle: one uninterrupted motion, left open rather than closed.
     Crossing it with a single bold figure follows the kamon (家紋) crest tradition — one enclosing
     ring, one mark inside, nothing else.

     The circle is a filled outline rather than a stroked arc so the brush can taper, thin at the
     start, swelling through the sweep, thinning into the tail. A uniform-width ring reads as a
     washer.

     The group scale keeps everything inside the circle an adaptive mask can crop to, so this
     survives round, squircle and squared-off launchers from one drawing. -->
